#lang saffron
;; saffron-lang-plan S7 / D5: the Saffron -> Turmeric boundary is a CONTRACT
;; boundary -- an implicit CHECKED cast at each argument whose parameter is
;; concrete and whose argument is `any`.
;;
;; Three options were considered and (c) chosen: rejecting unless the caller
;; annotates is safe and unusable; ERASING -- passing the payload word through
;; unchecked -- turns a type error into a memory-safety bug, because the word is
;; whatever the box held; so the seam CHECKS.  It reuses `elab_any_unbox_to`,
;; the node `(cast x T)` already lowers to, rather than inventing a mechanism --
;; which is why a mismatch panics with the ordinary `cast: any holds ...`
;; message and why the hand-written spelling and the compiler-inserted one are
;; the same operation.
;;
;; This fixture is the passing half: the values really are the declared types,
;; so every call goes through.  tests/fixtures/saffron-boundary-panic is the
;; other half.
;;
;; The elements come out of a `[...]` literal on purpose -- that is a
;; `(Vec any)` after the S6 widen, so each `vec-get` really is an `any` at the
;; call site and the seam really fires.  Passing a bare literal would type-check
;; statically and prove nothing.

(defn takes-int [x : int] : int (+ x 1))
(defn takes-cstr [s : cstr] : cstr s)
(defn takes-float [f : float] : float (* f 2.0))

(defn main []
  (let [v [7 "seven" 3.5]]
    (println (takes-int (vec-get v 0)))
    (println (takes-cstr (vec-get v 1)))
    (println (takes-float (vec-get v 2)))
    (vec-free v))
  0)
